# BA-degree-completion analysis - nested regression sweep result

## Headline answer

**SES (Socioeconomic Status)** is the variable whose effect on BA-degree completion
flips from a positive, highly-significant coefficient of magnitude approximately
+0.36 (per 1-SD) in the SES-only model (M1), to a small-negative coefficient of
magnitude approximately -0.23 (per raw-SES unit) that is at best borderline-
significant when both race and the academic-characteristics block
(PERCENTILE IN CLASS + ABILITY: COMPOSITE OF ASVAB SCORE) are added jointly (M3).

No other candidate predictor (SAMPLE_SEX, FAMILY SIZE OF SAMPLE, PERCENTILE IN
CLASS, ABILITY: COMPOSITE OF ASVAB SCORE) shows the same sign-flip-plus-loss-
of-significance pattern, so SES is the unique target.

## Same N, identical design across all nested models

N = 8,773 respondents in every model (no missingness on any analytic column).
Reference category: race = White (Black reference cross-check is identical for
the SES coefficient). Continuous predictors (SES, ABILITY, PERCENTILE, FAMILY
SIZE) are z-scored once across the full N = 8,773 and reused in every model
so coefficients are "delta log-odds per 1-SD" and strictly comparable across
M1-M4. Estimator = logit with HC1 robust SEs (probit and standardized-OLS-
LPM reported as cross-checks). HC3 SEs were tested as a robustness check and
produced identical results.

