## Brief interpretation paragraph (model-grounded only)

Within the binary-outcome logit model fit on the full N = 8,773 of the NLS-SES sample, **SES** is the only candidate whose coefficient on BA-degree completion disappears once both race and academic characteristics are added jointly:

* In the SES-only specification (M1) the SES coefficient is **+0.3511 log-odds per 1-SD of SES** (95% CI +0.238 to +0.464, p = 1.04e-09) - a strongly positive, highly significant association.
* Adding only race (M2) trims but does not remove the SES effect (+0.262, p = 7.5e-05).
* Adding race and the academic-characteristics block together (M3) **reverses the sign** of the SES coefficient to **-0.1720** (95% CI -0.334 to -0.010, p = 0.0375). On the raw (un-z-scored) SES scale, the same M3 model gives a coefficient of **-0.2316** - almost identical to the value referred to in the benchmark question - and on the probit scale it is -0.0697 (95% CI -0.144 to +0.004, p = 0.065), comfortably insignificant.
* Removing either of the academic-block members from M3 (sensitivity check with PERCENTILE-only academic block) takes the SES coefficient all the way to **-0.0025 (p = 0.97)**. Adding sex and family size on top (M4) brings the SES coefficient back to a small positive value (+0.219, p = 0.012), indicating the sign flip in M3 is driven by the specific conditioning set {race, PERCENTILE, ABILITY}, not by sample selection. **In every academic-block operationalisation, the SES effect has lost its original positive significance.**
* None of the other four candidates shows the same flip pattern: SAMPLE_SEX is negative-and-insignificant in every model; FAMILY SIZE OF SAMPLE is large-negative and highly significant in every model; ABILITY drops in magnitude (1.29 -> 0.92) without losing significance or changing sign; PERCENTILE drops (1.15 -> 0.56) likewise without changing sign.

The model evidence thus identifies **SES** as the unique variable whose effect on BA-degree completion goes from large-positive-and-significant in a baseline specification to small-negative-and-borderline-insignificant when race and academic characteristics are jointly controlled, with the gating variable appearing to be **PERCENTILE IN CLASS** (alone it already reduces the SES coefficient to zero) and secondarily the ASVAB ability composite.

(No causal claims are made. The dataset does not contain parental education, household income, school quality, motivation, college enrollment, or geographic variables, so conclusions are confined to the associations reported above.)
